  "tour": {
    "title": "When Prayer Won’t Come: A Listening Tour Through Grief",
    "intro": "You are only three weeks into a loss, and this tour will not ask you to make peace with it on schedule. It begins with quiet companionship, stays with Jesus at a graveside, then offers lament—not forced positivity—as a possible language when prayer feels impossible, before asking where God might be in suffering without pretending to explain why your mother died.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "forty-minutes-ot:podcast:444302a2-d904-43b3-a21f-4c7fb0a30dd7",
        "startSec": 764,
        "endSec": 821,
        "why": "Begin with company rather than explanation. The teachers say that, at first, it is better to “just sit with them in it,” and one speaker remembers that when Luke died, “it wasn’t the words much that mattered,” but that people were there. This is a brief permission not to have words right now.",
        "episodeTitle": "Job 2:11-3:26 (Episode 387)",
        "sourceId": "forty-minutes-ot",
        "source": "40 Minutes in the Old Testament",
        "audioUrl": "https://adbarker.com/stream/tfVE3XBxC9JCmb97VsZt7vpT/traffic.libsyn.com/secure/40minot/40_ep_387.mp3?dest-id=245197",
        "durationSec": 2435,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:a1a084d1-com-06ada9f3-4c3f-3851-9ba5-35f817a4ee6f",
        "startSec": 1307,
        "endSec": 1507,
        "why": "This stays close to grief without making Jesus emotionally distant from it. The speaker describes Mary saying, “Lord, if you had been here, our brother wouldn’t have died,” then says Jesus is “deeply agitated” by death and its pain before dwelling on the simple words, “Jesus wept.”",
        "episodeTitle": "John 11:1-54",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/rhu2ccvr4rgf5c54/john_111-54bhwxv.mp3",
        "durationSec": 2212,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "spoken-gospel:podcast:aa93fe68-dfc1-11ee-8d8c-1b5a7e037e3d",
        "startSec": 3392,
        "endSec": 3532,
        "why": "For the days when you cannot compose a prayer, this clip offers lament as honest speech rather than spiritual failure. It says, “Give yourself permission to lament,” and suggests putting words such as “look and see my suffering” into your own mouth, because “God can handle it.”",
        "episodeTitle": "Lamentations Overview: Mercy Every Morning",
        "sourceId": "spoken-gospel",
        "source": "Spoken Gospel",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/SPG8477051140.mp3?updated=1710173642",
        "durationSec": 3616,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:94aafd63-com-ed4952df-b7f0-3e15-be5c-b4ceb800e7a9",
        "startSec": 1205,
        "endSec": 1427,
        "why": "End with a careful answer to “where is God?” rather than a tidy reason for the loss. Wright points to “the Jesus who weeps at the tomb,” and to the Spirit “groaning within us,” while explicitly admitting, “I haven’t answered the question.” His emphasis is God with people in trouble, not an explanation that makes grief disappear.",
        "episodeTitle": "#51 Pain and suffering Part 1",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR6696807751.mp3?updated=1751028726",
        "durationSec": 1838,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      }
    ],
    "closing": "You do not have to turn these clips into a conclusion, or even into a prayer. For now, perhaps it is enough to be accompanied, to hear that tears and protest belong in the biblical story, and to borrow words—or silence—until your own begin to return.",
    "totalSeconds": 619
  },
